{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1789229494,"nonce":"0LSKH8eX5IGGAfRmSQ9mcMmTKNM=","message":"Fixed in 94bdca5 (air) and d48c04a (ash).\n\nThe fixture (TestVecArrayAddr) could not be written until the widener widened something: Haxe compiles `i = i + 1` and `i++` to OIncr, which the transform declines (UnscalableInductionStep), so nothing in the corpus had ever widened. With `i = 1 + i` the first real widening exposed, besides the Array addressing this issue is about:\n\n- retime_induction looked for the step Int in the back-edge Add's own block as its right operand; after LICM it is in the preheader and `1 + i` puts it on the left, so the vector loop stepped by 1 and read past the array (interp: \"GetArray: index 64 out of bounds\"). Found by definition now, with a fresh Int before the Add; a back value with any other reader (GVN merging `a[i + 1]` with the step) refuses.\n- every scalar the pass minted reused the induction variable's register, so the serializer emitted `And r3 = r3 \u0026 r3`; `new_reg` per value.\n- the serializer copied phis by `value_reg`, which every vector value shares, so a vector phi got no copies; lanes are preallocated and copied lane by lane.\n- wire_epilogue and collapse_reduction rewrote post-loop instrs and terminators but not phis (`return flag ? i : -1` returned vend).\n- LLVM: emit_vector_binop did into_int_value() on a \u003c4 x i32\u003e (inkwell panic); VecLoad/VecStore carried the vector type's align 16 on 4-aligned varray data (movdqa faults on x86-64); the host VARRAY_DATA_OFFSET was used instead of target_abi.varray_data_offset() (16 on wasm32).\n- --emit-optimized dropped Serialized::new_ints, so an emitted widened program died with \"Int operand N names no constant\"; minted constants are appended to the pool (test emit_optimized.rs).\n\nVerified: TestVecArrayAddr on interp, ASH_AIR=off, ASH_AIR=v2-serialize, --mode jit, hybrid cranelift/llvm at threshold 1, AOT (aarch64 and x86-64 NUC); the review's probes (a[i+1], post-loop phi, two widened loops sharing an accumulator) all OK on every engine; air tests 182/182; parity matrix; NUC conformance unit+sys 1069/1069 on aot, LLVM-pinned hybrid, interp and jit.\n\nStill open on the widener side: OIncr-stepped loops (every `for`) are never widened.","files":null}]}